--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What was taken, and where it lives now
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

No graphify source was copied. graphify is ~2MB of Python across forty
tree-sitter grammars; this is POSIX sh and awk. Everything below was
reimplemented from reading it.

Adapted:

  graphify/export.py to_json, ARCHITECTURE.md extraction schema
    -> scripts/context-graph.awk
    The node/edge record shape, and the per-edge confidence tag
    (EXTRACTED / INFERRED, with OBSERVED added here for the activity ledger).
    Tagging every edge with how it was learned is graphify's best idea: it
    makes the difference between "this link is written down" and "I matched
    it in prose" visible at the point of use rather than in a footnote.

  graphify/export.py to_obsidian
    -> scripts/context-vault.awk
    One note per node with [[wikilinks]], kind tags in frontmatter, and an
    index note. Most importantly the ownership manifest: a file present in
    the target directory but absent from the manifest belongs to the user and
    is never overwritten. graphify learned that one by clobbering people's
    vaults (its issues #1506, #2863), and the rule is worth inheriting
    without repeating the lesson.

  graphify/cli.py _bash_invokes_search
    -> the `signature` function in hooks/context-kit.sh and
       Get-CommandSignature in hooks/context-kit.ps1
    Deciding what a shell command actually runs from its executed tokens
    rather than a substring scan: drop heredoc bodies and quoted spans, take
    the first command segment, skip VAR=value prefixes and wrapper words with
    their own flags. Used here to give one procedure one signature, so
    `pytest -k x`, `sudo pytest -q` and `env CI=1 pytest` reach the repeat
    threshold together instead of separately.

  graphify/cli.py _mark_session_denied
    -> claim_once in hooks/context-kit.sh
    Claiming a one-time action through an atomic filesystem primitive
    (O_EXCL there, mkdir here) rather than test-then-write, so a nudge fires
    at most once and two subagents starting together cannot both claim it.

  graphify/reflect.py
    -> scripts/findings.awk, skills/finding-ledger/SKILL.md
    The whole work-memory model: outcome tags (useful / dead_end /
    corrected), a signed and time-decayed score per cited source, a
    corroboration floor before anything is called preferred, the preferred /
    tentative / contested / dead-end / corrections split, determinism with no
    model in the loop, and the --if-stale no-op that makes it cheap to run
    every session. The reader differs: graphify files one document per saved
    answer, while this reads the append-only per-investigation logs that
    findings/README.md already prescribed, so the existing convention did not
    have to change.

  graphify/serve.py serve_http
    -> the serve subcommand in scripts/context-graph.sh
    Bind loopback by default and warn loudly and specifically before binding
    wider.

  tools/skillgen (the split core + references/ layout)
    -> docs/authoring.md, skills/skill-capture/SKILL.md, and applied to
       skills/context-cleanup/
    Keeping a skill's decision path in SKILL.md and its lookup material in a
    references/ sidecar loaded on demand. This is the three-tier model
    applied inside a skill; graphify ships the structure, and it is the right
    structure.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What was deliberately not taken
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Recorded because the reasons are the interesting part, and because someone
will otherwise propose them again.

  The code index (extract.py, ~380KB, forty tree-sitter grammars).
    This plugin does not index code, on purpose. Code structure is in the
    code and an agent can read it. What context-graph maps is the context
    layout, which is the part nothing else can see.

  benchmark.py, and the token-reduction figure it prints.
    Its baseline is `corpus_words = nodes * 50` whenever a real word count is
    not supplied, and the headline ratio divides by it. A fabricated
    denominator is worse than no number, because the number gets quoted.
    /context and /shortcut-ledger produce real counts; use those.

  The unpkg vis-network dependency in the HTML export.
    A viewer that fetches a script from a CDN is a blank page on an
    air-gapped machine. templates/graph/viewer.html draws to a canvas with
    nothing loaded from anywhere, and scripts/check.sh fails the build if
    that ever stops being true.

  The MCP server (serve.py, ~127KB).
    A context graph has tens of nodes. Reading graph.json costs less than
    standing up a protocol to query it.

  The 41KB single-file skill, and the twenty-plus per-platform installers.
    A context-efficiency tool whose own skill is 41KB is the failure mode
    this plugin exists to argue against. The split was worth taking; the size
    was not.

  Neo4j, FalkorDB, GraphML, Cypher and SVG exports.
    No audience here.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Divergence
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is an absorption, not a dependency. context-kit does not track graphify
upstream and will not receive its fixes.

graphify is a different tool solving a different problem, and a good one. If
what you want is a queryable map of a large codebase — cross-file call
graphs, community detection, query/path/explain over forty languages — use
graphify. It is not a competitor to this; the two answer different questions
and run happily side by side.
